* ''Doorstep quick, ford full'' (operator, 2026-11-18, after both controllers talked it out: //"doorstep quick, ford full"//): a push to a lane branch (`develop--*`, `lane/*`) runs CI's Quick gates alone; the full battery runs on the queue's `merge_group`, on each push to `develop` and `main` (and to any other branch), on a fork's pull request, and by `workflow_dispatch` for a lane that wants a whole reading before it queues. The ground runs measured: the account runs about 20 jobs at once, each lane push raised the whole ten-job battery, and the queue's runs waited behind lane runs for hours. The ruleset's four required checks still report on a lane head: `Quick gates` runs, `Test` and `Witnesses` report skipped, and `E2E / Merge shard reports` walks its one doorstep step and passes (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`'s header; `tools/ci-graph.test.mjs` D1-D3 hold the run law). ''The canon doorstep'': a push that changes only the canon paths `tools/ci-ignored-paths.json` names runs that same doorstep set, on a lane or on `develop` and `main` alike: CI's push trigger ignores no path, and its `Scope` job reads the push against that one list (`develop` and `main` against the push's own `before`, any other branch against its merge base with `develop`), so a canon pull request reports the four checks and enters the queue, and a canon push to `develop` raises the scope read, the Quick gates and the doorstep step, never the battery; a mixed push reads as code, `merge_group` runs whole, and a run in flight on `develop` or `main` finishes beside a newer push (`tools/ci-graph.test.mjs` D4-D6, `tools/ci-ignored-paths.test.mjs`).

